.TH AENIGMA-TOR 8 "2026-08-17" "aenigma 5.0.0" "Aenigma Administration Scripts"
.SH NAME
aenigma-tor \- configure and publish a Tor onion (hidden) service for an aenigma-managed backend
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B aenigma-tor
.B \-l
.I LOCAL_ADDRESS
.B \-o
.I ONION_PORT
.B \-s
.I SERVICE_NAME
.RB [ \-u
.IR AUTHORIZED_USER ]
.RB [ \-c
.IR CONFIG_ADDRESS ]
.br
.B aenigma-tor
.B \-h
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B aenigma-tor
installs and enables the Tor daemon if necessary, provisions a
.B HiddenServiceDir
for the named service, appends the corresponding
.B HiddenServiceDir
/
.B HiddenServicePort
block to
.IR /etc/tor/torrc " (unless the service is already configured there),"
restarts Tor, waits for the onion address to be generated, optionally
configures client authorization for it, and prints the resulting address.
.PP
The script is intended as a basic, one-port-per-service starting point for
publishing a single local endpoint as a Tor onion service. It is not a
general-purpose torrc manager: if a service directory named by
.I SERVICE_NAME
already has a matching
.B HiddenServiceDir
line in
.IR /etc/tor/torrc ,
that block is left untouched on subsequent runs. To change a service's
port or target address, edit
.I /etc/tor/torrc
manually (or remove the relevant block) before re-running.
.PP
.B aenigma-tor
must be run as root, since it installs packages, edits
.IR /etc/tor/torrc ,
manages the
.B tor
systemd service, and changes ownership of files under
.IR /var/lib/tor .
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-l " LOCAL_ADDRESS"
The local address and port where the backend service is already listening,
e.g.
.IR 127.0.0.1:8080 .
This becomes the target of the
.B HiddenServicePort
line. Required.
.TP
.BI \-o " ONION_PORT"
The virtual port on which the onion service should accept connections from
Tor clients, e.g.
.IR 80 .
Required.
.TP
.BI \-s " SERVICE_NAME"
Name used to identify the onion service. This determines the service
directory,
.IR /var/lib/tor/ SERVICE_NAME,
which stores the service's private key and generated
.I hostname
(the \fB.onion\fR address). Required.
.TP
.BI \-u " AUTHORIZED_USER"
Optional. If given, grants the named user client authorization for this
service by invoking
.BR aenigma-tor-auth (8)
with
.BI \-s " SERVICE_NAME"
.BI \-u " AUTHORIZED_USER"
after the onion address has been generated (see
.SM NOTES ).
.TP
.BI \-c " CONFIG_ADDRESS"
Optional. If set to
.IR 1 ,
the resulting onion address is written into the aenigma configuration via
.BR aenigma-config (8)
under the
.I OnionService
key, as
.IR http://<address>.onion .
If set to
.I 0
or omitted, the aenigma configuration is left unchanged.
.TP
.B \-h
Display usage information and exit.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success; the onion service address was generated and printed.
.TP
.B 1
Invoked without root privileges, called with insufficient or invalid
arguments, or the onion service address failed to appear within the
generation timeout.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /etc/tor/torrc
Tor's main configuration file. A
.B HiddenServiceDir
/
.B HiddenServicePort
block is appended here for each new service.
.TP
.IB /var/lib/tor/ SERVICE_NAME
Per-service directory created and owned by the
.I debian-tor
user, mode 0700. Contains the service's private key and, once Tor has
published the descriptor, the
.I hostname
file holding the
.B .onion
address.
.SH NOTES
.IP \(bu 2
The script performs a literal, whole-line match against
.I /etc/tor/torrc
to decide whether a service is already configured; it does not attempt to
merge, update, or deduplicate ports for an existing
.BR HiddenServiceDir .
.IP \(bu 2
Directory ownership and permissions under
.IB /var/lib/tor/ SERVICE_NAME
are (re)applied on every run, even if the directory already existed, so
that permission drift does not silently break Tor.
.IP \(bu 2
After configuration, the script waits up to 120 seconds for Tor to publish
the service and write the
.I hostname
file. On slow hosts or first-time descriptor publication this may take
close to the full timeout.
.IP \(bu 2
When
.B \-u
is given, client authorization is configured only after the
.I hostname
file is confirmed to exist, since
.BR aenigma-tor-auth (8)
embeds the onion address in the private key file it generates. This
ordering matters specifically for a service's first-ever run with
.BR \-u :
the address does not exist before Tor has been restarted and finished
publishing the descriptor.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR tor (1),
.BR torrc (5),
.BR systemctl (1),
.BR aenigma-tor-auth (8),
.BR aenigma-config (8)
.SH AUTHOR
Romulus-Emanuel Ruja <romulus.rujas@aenigma.ro>